    "turn_idx": 16,
    "turn_text": "Yeah. So I mean, it dovetails with maybe You're earlier comment here that you know we we need real people right in the farm bill real farmers We need the farm bill to be written by farmers for farmers by rural America for rural America in order for that to happen You know people like me can't just go into this assuming that we know all the answers We have to reach out to folks all over this country and and really just gain input and and gain their advice and have conversations with folks that the farm bill actually you know is a part of their life so Where we're at in the process right now is we're in that kind of end stages of kind of those conversations and getting to the committee work in regards to potentially in the next few months starting to mark up on the farm bill. So right now it is out and about listening to folks, gathering input, having field hearings, We've had committee hearings in Texas, Florida, California, Pennsylvania, listening sessions throughout the country. And so that's really a good thing. And again, just knowing my background, it's something that I've always felt really important. And I'm just really proud that the Chairman, G.T. Thompson sees that's important, that we need public policy that reflects the folks that it's trying to serve. In order to do that, we have to make sure we're having conversations with those folks. You know, I'm a corn and soybean farmer from Southern Minnesota. I understand that component of agriculture. I own a soil laboratory with my family. I understand the precision agriculture, the soil science, the environmental work that we as farmers are doing every day. But I'm not going to pretend to know how to, you know, how to grow cattle or how to grow cotton or what an almond farm looks like or what a pistachio farm looks like. So I need to myself make sure that I'm asking those questions, talking to folks that are living in those worlds, and then making sure that we're passing policy that reflects it. So the Farm Bill, we have a unique opportunity because I think most would agree in egg country or rural America that the Farm Bill that was passed in 2018 was a pretty good Farm Bill. And rarely in politics or in government, do we get to start at a place that was pretty good? And so now we can work around the edges to make it a little bit better and prove with the times. But we're by no means starting from scratch. So I think that's a good position to start from.",
