  {
    "episode_id": 96836,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96836.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 18,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, so just the mechanics, the funding mechanics overall, over 82% of the farm bill is in the nutrition title, which is SNAP, you know, the food programs. So there's always opportunities for education when it comes to that. You know, a lot of folks still call it the old food stamp program and it hasn't been called the food stamp program for quite some time. But that's what people know SNAP, the supplemental nutrition program to be. So the education component of what that really is and you know that that's going to be a big part of the farm bill but the other piece is you know protecting some of the core risk management tools that the farm bill really gives farmers. So crop insurance for instance you know one of the number one tools for my perspective that has really provided that risk management tool for farmers on the front end when you're having conversations with your lender on the back end when you're making marketing decisions. It provides you a lot more confidence and a lot more risk management to understand where your risks are and how you're protected and at what level. So crop insurance has been great that way. It also is probably one of the number one young farmer tools I think that we have at our disposal right now because there's not too many industries or programs where you have really that income insurance program that allows you to manage risk, it allows you to plan for the worst, hope for the best, make good marketing decisions, and really stay in the game for the next cropping cycle. And I look at it from, again, the fourth generation that's raising the fifth generation, It's a great tool that provides me opportunities to get to that fifth generation. So that's going to be the big piece. Conservation title of the Farm Bill is always something that, you know, weighs heavily on egg country. We want to make sure that that program, you know, stays strong, but it also stays voluntary. We hear loud and clear from folks in egg country that, you know, the conservation programs are great tools for vulnerable land. But don't, you know, don't make us do it in a way that ties our hands and puts us at a disadvantage. Don't tie up our land. That's good production land. Let's make sure that it's directed towards vulnerable farm land and whatnot. So that's kind of the big conversation pieces that I'm hearing as we're talking to farmers and rural folks from all over the country. I would close kind of a thought with, we also have to remember that USDA is a big agency. Over 100,000 employees So it's not just farm program at FSA, but it's also rural development. So we talk about a lot of these rural communities, water, wastewater, rural infrastructure, small business, broadband, high-speed internet, the ReConnect program. That's all through USDA, and that's all part of this reauthorization of the farm bill. So all very important things to rural America.",
