  {
    "episode_id": 96836,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96836.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 20,
    "turn_text": "You know, I worry a little bit that there's more and more of a disconnect between what the legislative branch does and what the executive branch does in regards to the rules and regulations and the implementation of certain rules and regulations. So you look at the waiver process for E15, for instance. You know, the legislative intent is pretty clear that E15 is a strong, viable energy source. We want rural America to be a part of our America First Energy conversation. And everything is sailing right along, and then the EPA gets involved in regards to the waiver and the summer use of it. So I think that we have to be clear what we want to do legislatively, and we have to be clear not just to the folks that the Farm Bill will serve, but also the regulators and the agency folks that are going to have to implement it. I would say there's ripe opportunity for better conversations with the regulators in regards to the implementation of the Farm Bill. We could pass the greatest Farm Bill with the best policies, the best laws, and if they're manipulated and massaged through the regulatory process, we and farm country don't really see the results that we're looking for in regards to this policy. So there is opportunities for us to strengthen that. And I think overall, That's where you see a lot of disconnect and maybe some folks that are kind of sick of politics or government as usual because we see some of the lack of common sense when it comes to the regulatory side, specifically in the implementation or the enforcement. So we can do better. We owe it to our kids and future generations to do better when it comes to the administrative state of our government.",
